---
/*
  Site analytics, ticket T18.

  PostHog, in a NEW project inside the org that already carries the extension's
  telemetry, so site traffic and product telemetry stay separate streams while
  staying one tool. Config comes from the environment and never from the repo:

    PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY    the project key
    PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST   defaults to https://us.i.posthog.com

  The key is the EXTENSION's project, shared on purpose: PostHog's free plan
  allows one project per organisation. Everything from here is stamped
  source: 'site' so the two streams stay separable. If the plan ever allows a
  second project, split them and drop the marker.

  With no key set, this component renders nothing at all. That is the local and
  preview default, and it means a missing key is a quiet no-op rather than a
  console error on every page.

  Two things about PostHog that are easy to get wrong and are worth writing down
  here, because both have already cost this project time:

  1. Ingestion answers 200 "Ok" for ANY key, valid or not. So a bad key fails
     silently and looks exactly like success. The only way to confirm events are
     landing is to watch the Activity feed in the PostHog UI. Never conclude from
     a 200 that the wiring works.
  2. `persistence: 'memory'` keeps this cookieless, which is why the site owes no
     consent banner. Changing it changes that, so do not change it casually.

  3. The loader body below is PLAIN TEXT, and has to stay that way. `is:inline`
     tells Astro to emit the script verbatim, so it does not evaluate template
     expressions inside it. This body used to be wrapped in {`...`}, which
     therefore shipped the braces and backticks to the browser as source: the
     whole loader parsed as a block containing one discarded string and never
     ran. It failed silently for as long as it existed, because a string
     expression statement is perfectly legal JavaScript and throws nothing.
     `define:vars` is unaffected and is what injects key and host above.
*/
const key = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY;

/*
  SAME-ORIGIN BY DEFAULT, and this is the whole reason anything is recorded.

  Pointed straight at us.i.posthog.com, the library never loaded for anyone
  running a content blocker: the request for array.js came back 204 with an
  empty body, so `window.posthog.init` stayed undefined and not one event —
  not even a pageview — was ever sent. Measured in a real browser, not guessed.
  The audience here is developers, so that is not an edge case.

  Blockers match on the hostname. `/ingest` is first-party, so it passes.
  website/vercel.json rewrites it to PostHog: /ingest/static/* to the assets
  host, everything else to the ingestion host.

  The rewrite is a VERCEL feature. `astro dev` does not have it, so /ingest
  404s locally — which costs nothing, because a local build has no key and this
  component renders nothing at all. Set PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST to an absolute URL
  to bypass the proxy.
*/
const host = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST ?? '/ingest';
---

{
  key && (
    <script is:inline define:vars={{ key, host }}>
      (function () {
        /*
          NOTHING MAY ASSIGN window.posthog BEFORE array.js RUNS.

          This used to install a small stub there so early clicks could queue.
          array.js then loaded, saw the name already taken, and left it alone —
          so `window.posthog.init` never existed, the onload handler returned at
          its own guard, and the library never initialized. Measured live: the
          script fetched 200 and window.posthog was still {capture, __queue}.

          The queue lives here instead, in a local array the library cannot
          collide with, and drains once init has run. Same guarantee, no stub.
        */
        var pending = [];
        var ready = false;

        var s = document.createElement('script');
        s.async = true;
        s.src = host + '/static/array.js';
        s.onload = function () {
          if (!window.posthog || !window.posthog.init) return;
          window.posthog.init(key, {
            api_host: host,
            // Where the PostHog UI lives. api_host is our own /ingest path now,
            // and without this the SDK builds "view in PostHog" links against
            // it and they go nowhere.
            ui_host: 'https://us.posthog.com',
            // Cookieless: no consent banner owed. See the note above.
            persistence: 'memory',
            capture_pageview: true,
            capture_pageleave: true,
            autocapture: true,
            // The site is public marketing. Nothing here is sensitive, but
            // masking inputs by default keeps the waitlist address out of
            // session data if replay is ever turned on.
            mask_all_text: false,
            mask_all_element_attributes: false,
            // The waitlist stores addresses as survey responses, but the site
            // renders its own form and PostHog must never draw one over it.
            // Without this the SDK will pop the survey widget on top of the
            // page as soon as its targeting matches, and keeping it away by
            // writing targeting rules that never match is a rule you have to
            // remember forever. Responses still record: this disables the UI,
            // not the capture.
            disable_surveys: true,
          });
          // Every event this site sends carries source: 'site'.
          //
          // PostHog's free plan allows one project, and that one already holds
          // the extension's telemetry, so the site shares it. Without a marker
          // the two streams are one pile: a funnel over "did they install"
          // would silently mix people who clicked the button on this page with
          // people who were already running the extension.
          //
          // register() makes it a super property, so it rides on $pageview and
          // autocapture too, not just the named events below. Filter on it and
          // the two products come apart again.
          window.posthog.register({ source: 'site' });

          ready = true;
          for (var i = 0; i < pending.length; i++) {
            window.posthog.capture(pending[i][0], pending[i][1] || {});
          }
          pending.length = 0;
        };
        document.head.appendChild(s);

        // The named events the funnel reads. Delegated from the document so a
        // section rendered later still reports, and so there is exactly one
        // listener rather than one per button.
        function send(name, props) {
          if (ready && window.posthog && window.posthog.capture) {
            window.posthog.capture(name, props || {});
          } else {
            // Before init, or if array.js never arrives. Held, not dropped:
            // the waitlist submit is the one event most likely to happen while
            // the library is still in flight.
            pending.push([name, props]);
          }
        }

        document.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
          var el = e.target && e.target.closest ? e.target.closest('[data-analytics]') : null;
          if (!el) return;
          var name = el.getAttribute('data-analytics');
          if (!name) return;
          var props = {};
          for (var i = 0; i < el.attributes.length; i++) {
            var a = el.attributes[i];
            if (a.name.indexOf('data-analytics-') === 0) {
              props[a.name.slice('data-analytics-'.length)] = a.value;
            }
          }
          send(name, props);
        });

        // The escape hatch for the events that are not clicks: a form submit,
        // for one. A page fires document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent(
        // 'site-analytics', { detail: { name, props } })) and stays ignorant of
        // PostHog, which is what keeps the no-key build free of any reference to
        // it rather than merely free of the loader.
        document.addEventListener('site-analytics', function (e) {
          if (!e.detail || !e.detail.name) return;
          send(e.detail.name, e.detail.props);
        });
      })();
    </script>
  )
}
